  "text": "Our Team – Flint Animal Cancer Center\nSkip to content\ncolorado state\nuniversity\nCSU\nflint animal\ncancer center\nAbout Us\nOur Story\nFACC Team\nContact Us\nNews\nFor Owners\nOur Services\nCancer Information Request\nAppointments\nTreatment Options\nPreparing for Your Visit\nFrequently Asked Questions\nFor DVMs\nConsult Line\nTreatment Options\nOne Cure\nAbout One Cure\nRacing for One Cure\nDonate to One Cure\nClinical Trials\nAbout Clinical Trials\nCurrent Clinical Trials\nEducation & Research\nComparative Oncology Research\nOncology Specialty Training\nDonate\nGreatest Need\nPatient Assistance\nOne Cure\nScholarship\nResearch\nFeline Cancer Research\nFACC Friends\nmenu\nclose\nCategory:\nOur Team\nsearch\nread more\nOur Team\nFACC’s Global Connections\nLauren Mingus\n|\nDecember 3, 2019\nSince the early 1980s, the Flint Animal Cancer Center has trained more surgical, medical, and radiation oncologists than any other veterinary institution. Beyond the formal training program, each year, the FACC also welcomes dozens of guest observers from around the world. Over the years, this expanding collegial network has led to FACC’s growing global impact on […]\nread more\nOur Team\nDr. Dan Regan Searches For Answers To Combat Metastatic Disease\nLauren Mingus\n|\nAugust 8, 2019\nWhen Dr. Dan Regan talks about his work in cancer research, he boils it down to a simple analogy known as the seed and soil theory. He wants to understand how cancer cells (seeds) know where to find welcoming locations to take root (soil).  In science speak, Regan studies the tumor microenvironment and metastatic disease. […]\nread more\nOur Team\nVet Tech Team Brings Passion And Experience To Provide Unparalleled Care\nLauren Mingus\n|\nOctober 17, 2018\nOur veterinary technicians are critical members of the Flint Animal Cancer Center team. With up to 30 patient visits each day, they help manage a variety of responsibilities from check-in to administering treatment. While they share many of the same passions – a love of animals and finding a cure for cancer – our vet […]\nread more\nOne Cure Clinical Trials\nCanine Cancer Prevention Vaccine Study Seeks Participants\nLauren Mingus\n|\nJune 18, 2019\nAs part of the largest interventional canine clinical trial ever conducted, veterinarians leading the Vaccine Against Canine Cancer Study seek to enroll 800 healthy, middle-aged pet dogs to evaluate the effectiveness of a new cancer prevention vaccine. The clinical trials portion of the study is led by Colorado State University’s Dr. Douglas Thamm, director of […]\nread more\nOur Team\nThe Legacy of Dr. Rodney Page\nKate Shelton\n|\nMay 24, 2023\nThe answer to cancer may be walking right beside us. – Dr. Page The One Medicine for pets and people approach has guided Dr. Rodney Page’s career. After a lifetime of achievements, Page has announced his plans to retire as the longtime director of the Robert H. and Mary G. Flint Animal Cancer Center and […]\nread more\nOur Team\nHelping pet families navigate the cancer journey\nLauren Mingus\n|\nJune 2, 2021\nAs members of a land-grant institution, we believe it is our responsibility to share our knowledge and expertise. To fulfill that commitment, the Flint Animal Cancer Center Consult Service offers a free resource connecting pet parents and referring veterinarians with our oncology specialists. The program answers approximately 2,000 requests for oncology information annually. The service […]\nread more\nOur Team\nVeterinary Oncology Training Programs\nLauren Mingus\n|\nFebruary 19, 2021\nEducating future cancer specialists is central to the mission of the Flint Animal Cancer Center. We have trained more surgical, medical, and radiation oncologists than any other veterinary institution in the world. We also established the first Ph.D. program in cancer biology for  veterinarians. Until the day we conquer cancer, the need for additional clinical […]\nread more\nLatest News\nFACC scientists search for solutions to cancer treatment challenges\nLauren Mingus\n|\nMay 25, 2021\nWhile many scientists at the Flint Animal Cancer Center focus their research on cancer cells, the Laboratory of Comparative Musculoskeletal Oncology and Traumatology views cancer through an additional lens. “Many of our colleagues are doing great work focusing on cancer itself – how to treat it effectively, how to make sure it doesn’t spread,” said […]\nread more\nOur Team\nFlint Animal Cancer Center Focuses on Opportunities\nLauren Mingus\n|\nNovember 30, 2020\nAfter pivoting to address the crisis caused by the global pandemic last spring, the team at the Flint Animal Cancer Center is thinking of the future and reimagining what is possible for cancer research, treatment, and prevention. “Despite the pandemic, cancer hasn’t taken a break and neither has our team,” said Dr. Rodney Page, director, […]\nread more\nOur Team\nNew Fellowship Trains Future Veterinary Oncology Clinician-Scientists\nLauren Mingus\n|\nDecember 3, 2019\nWhen Dr. Samantha Schlemmer read the posting for the Seeker Oncology Research Fellowship at the Flint Animal Cancer Center, it was as if the stars had aligned. Schlemmer was nearing the end of her residency in clinical pathology at Texas A&M and considering her next steps when she learned about the research fellowship. “I was […]\nread more\nOur Team\nOncology 2019 – Growing To Meet The Needs Of Patients And Their Caretakers.\nDr. Rodney Page\n|\nDecember 19, 2018\nNo other area of companion animal health care has expanded as robustly as veterinary oncology.  In the last 30 years, veterinary oncology has developed from an interest group to a multi-specialty, international network of clinical professionals.  In total, there may now be more than 1,000 veterinarians that have achieved a formal credential in either medical […]\nread more\nLatest News\nCSU cancer researcher named 2020 Boettcher Investigator\nLauren Mingus\n|\nMay 28, 2020\nDan Regan, an assistant professor in Microbiology, Immunology, and Pathology, was recently selected to receive the Boettcher Foundation’s 2020 Webb-Waring Biomedical Research Award. The award supports promising early-career scientific investigators, allowi­ng them to establish their independent research and make it competitive for major federal and private grants. Recipients, known as Boettcher Investigators, are awarded $235,000 to sustain […]\n1\n2\n»\nbrowse articles by topic\nFACC Patient Stories\nLatest News\nOne Cure Clinical Trials\nOne Cure Patients\nOur Team\nPet Cancer Treatment\nResearch\nStories of Hope\nTeaching and Learning\nbrowse articles by tag\nBone Cancer\nBrain Tumor\nCancer Research\nCancer Stories\nCanine Cancer\nCanine Cancer Clinical Trials\nCanine Cancer Prevention Vaccine\ncanine clinical trial\nCanine Hemangiosarcoma\nCanine Leg Amputation\nCanine Lymphoma\nCanine Nasal Tumor\nCanine Osteosarcoma\nCanine Soft Tissue Sarcoma canine cancer clinical trials\nCaring for pets with cancer\ncat cancer\nChemotherapy\ncomparative oncology\nComparative Oncology Research\nDan Regan\nDiagnostic Imaging\nDiet for Pets with Cancer\nEmotional Support\nfeline cancer\nFeline GI Lymphoma\nFeline Lymphoma\nFlint Animal Cancer Center Staff\nGlioma\nGolden Retriever Lifetime Study\nHemangiosarcoma\nLaiks Fund\nlimb-spare surgery\nNasal Osteosarcoma\nNews\nOne Cure\nOne Health\nPatient Assistance Funds\nPatient Care\nPatient Stories\nPatient Story\nPet Cancer\nPet Cancer Comparative Oncology\nPet Cancer Patient Assistance Funds\nPet Cancer Prevention\nPet Cancer Terms\nPet Cancer Treatment\nPet Grief Counseling\npet leg amputation\nPet Loss\nPet Quality of Life\nPlasmacytomas\nRacing for One Cure\nradiation biology\nRadiation Bolus\nRadiation Oncology Research\nRadiation Therapy\nradioimmunology\nResearch\nSoft Tissue Sarcoma\nSpeeding up the Cure\nSurgery\nVeterinary Oncology\nVeterinary Oncology History\nVeterinary Oncology Specialist Training\nVeterinary Oncology Specialty Training\nVeterinary Oncol